# Binary multiplication. Two numbers, most significant bit first.
#
#   rw progs/binmul.rw '<1011*110>'   ->   1000010
#
# Shift and add, driven from the top of the multiplier: for each of its bits,
# double the accumulator, and if the bit was 1 add the multiplicand in.
# Doubling is free -- append a 0 -- so all the work is in the adding, and the
# adding has to leave the multiplicand standing, because the next bit needs it
# again. binadd.rw could eat its operands; this one cannot.
#
#   < multiplicand # $ multiplier | accumulator % >
#
# Two pointers do the work, and both are their own parking spaces. ^ sits at
# the multiplicand's right end while idle and walks left as @ during an add,
# emitting one traveller per bit without disturbing the bit. % sits at the
# accumulator's right end while idle and walks left during an add, holding the
# running carry in its identity: % is no carry, & is a carry.
#
# There is no program counter anywhere. What sequences the phases is rule
# order alone: every rule below can only fire because everything above it has
# run out of matches. The outer loop sits below the whole add, so it cannot
# take another multiplier bit until both pointers have walked home.

# bootstrap: * and > are consumed, so each of these fires exactly once
*->^$
>->|%

# --- a traveller arrives: one column of the addition ---
# accumulator digit, carried bit, carry in -> result digit, carry out
0T%->%0
1T%->%1
0U%->%1
1U%->&0
0T&->%1
1T&->&0
0U&->&0
1U&->&1

# the accumulator ran out of digits: the missing one is a 0, and it grows
|T%->|%0
|U%->|%1
|T&->|%1
|U&->|&0

# a traveller on its way right. It stops at % because nothing moves it past.
T0->0T
T1->1T
T$->$T
T|->|T
U0->0U
U1->1U
U$->$U
U|->|U

# --- the add: @ walks left through the multiplicand, one bit per column ---
# the bit is put back as @ steps over it, which is what keeps it for later
0@->@0T
1@->@1U
<@-><H

# multiplicand spent: ripple the last carry into the rest of the accumulator
0&->%1
1&->&0
|&->|%1

# both pointers walk home. Below the add, so neither leaves early.
%0->0%
%1->1%
H0->0H
H1->1H
H$->^$

# --- outer loop: one multiplier bit, most significant first ---
# D doubles the accumulator; E doubles it and sends F back to start an add
D0->0D
D1->1D
D|->|D
D%->0%
E0->0E
E1->1E
E|->|E
E%->F0%
0F->F0
1F->F1
|F->F|
$F->F$
^F->@

# take the next multiplier bit. Dead last in the outer loop: D, E and F all
# move by swapping, which shoves the next bit back against the $, so a launch
# placed any higher would fire again while a token was still in transit.
$0->$D
$1->$E

# --- multiplier spent: strip the multiplicand and the scaffolding ---
<0-><
<1-><
<^$|->[
[00->[0
[01->[1
[%->.0
%->
[->
